solution:
According to Thévenin's theorem, the equivalent emf is the open-circuit voltage across A and B

$$
\varepsilon_0 = V_{AB}
$$

$$
V_{AB} = \frac{6}{24+6} \times 15 \unit{V}
$$

$$
\varepsilon_0 = 3 \unit{V}
$$

The equivalent internal resistance is the resistance seen with the source shorted, i.e., the parallel combination of 6\unit{\ohm} and 24\unit{\ohm}

$$
r = \frac{6 \times 24}{6+24} \unit{\ohm}
$$

$$
r = \frac{24}{5} \unit{\ohm}
$$

The short-circuit current provided at the terminals is

$$
I = \frac{\varepsilon_0}{r}
$$

$$
I = \frac{3 \unit{V}}{\frac{24}{5} \unit{\ohm}}
$$

$$
I = \frac{5}{8} \unit{A}
$$

The pulsed source voltage is
$$
v_{in}(t)=u(t)-u(t-1)
$$
The unit-step response of the CR circuit is
$$
s(t)=u(t)e^{-t/RC}
$$
Therefore the output voltage of the CR circuit is
$$
v_0(t)=u(t)e^{-t/RC}-u(t-1)e^{-(t-1)/RC}
$$
For R=10^3\unit{\Omega} and RC=10^{-1}\unit{\mu s}
$$
R=10^3\unit{\Omega}
$$
$$
RC=10^{-1}\unit{\mu s}
$$
$$
v_0(t)=u(t)e^{-10t}-u(t-1)e^{-10(t-1)}
$$
For R=10^4\unit{\Omega} and RC=1\unit{\mu s}
$$
R=10^4\unit{\Omega}
$$
$$
RC=1\unit{\mu s}
$$
$$
v_0(t)=u(t)e^{-t}-u(t-1)e^{-(t-1)}
$$
For R=10^5\unit{\Omega} and RC=10\unit{\mu s}
$$
R=10^5\unit{\Omega}
$$
$$
RC=10\unit{\mu s}
$$
$$
v_0(t)=u(t)e^{-t/10}-u(t-1)e^{-(t-1)/10}
$$
All voltages are in \unit{V} and t is measured in \unit{\mu s}.

For silicon BJTs in saturation, the assumed voltage drops are
$$
V_{be(sat)} = 0.7 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_{ce(sat)} = 0.3 \unit{V}
$$
Case 1: With V_A = 4.0 V, T1 is saturated. Then T3 is also saturated. Therefore
$$
V_B = 0.7 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_E = 0.3 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_C = V_B + 0.3 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_C = 1.0 \unit{V}
$$
Case 2: With V_A = 0.2 V, T1 is in cutoff, so
$$
V_B = 0 \unit{V}
$$
Thus T3 is also in cutoff. T2 is saturated, hence
$$
V_C = 5 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_D = 5 \unit{V} - 0.7 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_D = 4.3 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_E = V_D - 0.7 \unit{V}
$$
$$
V_E = 3.6 \unit{V}
$$
